// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// app_color.slint — every app its own colour
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// This OS had one accent and painted everything with it. Every app glyph in the launcher,
// every header tile, every icon in the taskbar: the same teal, at the same weight. Sixteen
// applications and thirty screens, and no way to tell any of them apart at a glance — you had
// to read the label every single time, because the picture beside it carried no information.
//
// Monochrome is a legitimate choice and it was not a choice; it was the default falling
// through everywhere. The cost is that the desktop reads as one undifferentiated surface —
// competent and lifeless — which is the difference between an interface you can navigate by
// memory and one you have to parse.
//
// So colour becomes identity here, the way it is on every desktop worth comparing to: Mail is
// blue, Calendar is red, Notes is yellow, Music is pink. You learn the colour once and then
// stop reading labels. That is not decoration, it is a second channel of information running
// alongside the shape, and using only one of the two was leaving half the signal on the floor.
//
// Rules for anything added here:
//
//   Hue carries meaning where meaning exists. Anything about writing is warm, anything about
//   the system is cool, anything destructive stays with the danger colour rather than getting
//   a personality of its own.
//
//   Neighbours differ. The launcher sorts alphabetically, so Calendar and Containers land side
//   by side and must not be the same blue.
//
//   Readable on the dark surface first. This palette is tuned against #0c0c14, because that is
//   what the OS is nearly all of the time; the light theme takes the same hues darkened rather
//   than a second set, so there is one list to keep honest.
//
//   Loud only on a tile. The solid colour is for an app's ICON TILE — the launcher, the
//   desktop's workspace row and the taskbar (see AppTile). Everywhere else an app's colour is
//   a glyph or a faint tint, never a filled block (desk-and-mind, "bright and sharp").
//
// # One table
//
// `hue-for-app` is the only place an app is given a colour. It answers a hue NAME, and the
// three colours an app can wear — the glyph tone, the faint tint and the solid tile — are all
// read from that one name. The shell's Rust tests read this function too
// (`every_app_the_launcher_knows_has_a_colour` in crates/yantrik-ui/src/wire/app_grid.rs):
// an app the launcher can show and this table does not name fails the build rather than
// quietly falling back to the house accent.
import { Theme } from "theme.slint";
import { ThemeMode } from "theme.slint";

export global AppColor {
    // ── The palette ──
    //
    // Deliberately a fixed set rather than free-form per app: fourteen hues spaced around the
    // wheel, so two apps that land next to each other are always visibly different, and an app
    // added later picks one rather than inventing a shade nothing else uses.
    //
    // These are GLYPH tones: a stroke icon on a dark or light surface. Theme-aware.
    out property <color> blue:    ThemeMode.dark ? #4a9eff : #1f6fd0;
    // Files. A lighter, airier blue than Mail's, the colour a folder is on every desktop.
    out property <color> sky:     ThemeMode.dark ? #5cb0ff : #1570c8;
    out property <color> azure:   ThemeMode.dark ? #35c4e8 : #0f8ba8;
    out property <color> teal:    ThemeMode.dark ? #34d3b4 : #12907a;
    out property <color> green:   ThemeMode.dark ? #52d36a : #2a9440;
    out property <color> lime:    ThemeMode.dark ? #9ed44a : #5f8a1e;
    out property <color> yellow:  ThemeMode.dark ? #e8c44a : #9a7a10;
    // Notes. Warmer and deeper than yellow: a legal pad, not a highlighter.
    out property <color> amber:   ThemeMode.dark ? #f5b23c : #a0680a;
    out property <color> orange:  ThemeMode.dark ? #f0954a : #b8611a;
    out property <color> red:     ThemeMode.dark ? #f0655f : #c2352f;
    out property <color> pink:    ThemeMode.dark ? #f06aa8 : #b8306e;
    out property <color> magenta: ThemeMode.dark ? #cf6ae8 : #9330b0;
    out property <color> violet:  ThemeMode.dark ? #9a7cf0 : #5f42c0;
    out property <color> slate:   ThemeMode.dark ? #8a94a8 : #5a6274;

    // ── Tile fills ──
    //
    // The same hues as a SOLID tile with a glyph on it. Not theme-aware on purpose: an app
    // icon is an object with its own colour, like a real one on a real desk, and does not
    // change because the surface around it did. Each is dark enough to hold a white glyph at
    // 3:1 or better, except the three light hues (lime, yellow, amber), which carry a dark
    // glyph instead — see `on-tile`. AppTile's pre-rendered art is generated from these and from
    // `hue-for-app` (design/icons/generate.py): change one and run it, or the kit's tests fail.
    out property <color> tile-blue:    #2f6fe4;
    out property <color> tile-sky:     #2f8ae8;
    out property <color> tile-azure:   #1492c2;
    out property <color> tile-teal:    #0fa191;
    out property <color> tile-green:   #22a152;
    out property <color> tile-lime:    #8cc63a;
    out property <color> tile-yellow:  #f2c82e;
    out property <color> tile-amber:   #f6aa2a;
    out property <color> tile-orange:  #e8711f;
    out property <color> tile-red:     #e5484d;
    out property <color> tile-pink:    #e1457d;
    out property <color> tile-magenta: #ae45cf;
    out property <color> tile-violet:  #7a5af0;
    out property <color> tile-slate:   #5b6577;
    // The glyph on a light tile: a warm near-black, so it reads as ink on the colour rather
    // than a hole cut through it.
    out property <color> tile-ink:     #231a06;

    // ── Which app is which ──
    //
    // Keyed by the shell's own app ids, the same ones Icons.app is keyed by, so an app has one
    // identity rather than a picture from one table and a colour from another. Answers a hue
    // name from the palette above, or "" for an app this table does not know.
    //
    // The named ones come from the design (desk-and-mind, "Colour per app"): Files blue,
    // Calendar red, Notes amber, Terminal green, Mail blue, Browser teal, Studio violet. The
    // test beside the launcher holds those seven to it.
    public pure function hue-for-app(id: string) -> string {
        return id == "notes"          ? "amber"     // writing, warm
             : id == "editor"         ? "violet"
             : id == "documents"      ? "blue"
             : id == "spreadsheet"    ? "green"
             : id == "presentation"   ? "orange"
             : id == "snippets"       ? "magenta"

             : id == "email"          ? "blue"      // communication
             : id == "calendar"       ? "red"
             : id == "notifications"  ? "orange"

             : id == "music"          ? "pink"      // media
             : id == "media"          ? "pink"
             : id == "image"          ? "magenta"
             // Beside `image` on purpose but not the same colour: making and looking are one word
             // apart in the launcher and in a model's choice of app. Violet, because its
             // alphabetical neighbours there are spreadsheet (green) and system-monitor (lime).
             : id == "studio"         ? "violet"
             // Blender's own orange: the one app here whose colour people already know.
             : id == "blender"        ? "orange"
             : id == "arcade"         ? "orange"

             // The files, and the machine. Files is the folder blue; the rest are cool.
             //
             // Not teal: teal is a mind's colour (colour roles), so a wall of teal machine tiles
             // read as a wall of minds. Containers is the blue (its launcher neighbour Calendar
             // is red), Downloads the folder sky it saves into, Packages green. Browser alone
             // keeps teal, because the design names it so (`the_design_colours_hold`).
             : id == "files"          ? "sky"
             : id == "terminal"       ? "green"
             : id == "system"         ? "azure"
             : id == "sysmonitor"     ? "lime"
             // htop, installed with the OS: a process viewer, so System Monitor's lime with bars
             // of its own. Its theme icon was a photograph of a bar chart on the neutral plate,
             // the one picture in a row of flat glyphs (sign-off, 5 October).
             : id == "htop"           ? "lime"
             : id == "network"        ? "azure"
             : id == "containers"     ? "blue"
             : id == "packages"       ? "green"
             : id == "devices"        ? "slate"
             : id == "downloads"      ? "sky"
             : id == "browser"        ? "teal"
             : id == "weather"        ? "azure"

             : id == "memory"         ? "violet"    // the companion
             : id == "agents"         ? "violet"
             // Recipes: the one hue no other app wears, so a stage row reads as its own thing.
             : id == "recipes"        ? "yellow"
             : id == "bond"           ? "pink"
             : id == "personality"    ? "magenta"
             : id == "skills"         ? "lime"
             : id == "permissions"    ? "red"
             : id == "settings"       ? "slate"
             : id == "about"          ? "slate"
             : id == "launchpad"      ? "blue"
             : "";
    }

    /// A hue name's glyph tone.
    public pure function hue(name: string) -> color {
        return name == "blue"    ? root.blue
             : name == "sky"     ? root.sky
             : name == "azure"   ? root.azure
             : name == "teal"    ? root.teal
             : name == "green"   ? root.green
             : name == "lime"    ? root.lime
             : name == "yellow"  ? root.yellow
             : name == "amber"   ? root.amber
             : name == "orange"  ? root.orange
             : name == "red"     ? root.red
             : name == "pink"    ? root.pink
             : name == "magenta" ? root.magenta
             : name == "violet"  ? root.violet
             : name == "slate"   ? root.slate
             // Anything else — a third-party app, or one added without an entry here — takes
             // the shell's accent. It is the honest answer: we do not know what this is, so it
             // gets the house colour rather than a hue that would imply a category.
             : Theme.accent;
    }

    /// A hue name's solid tile fill.
    public pure function tile-hue(name: string) -> color {
        return name == "blue"    ? root.tile-blue
             : name == "sky"     ? root.tile-sky
             : name == "azure"   ? root.tile-azure
             : name == "teal"    ? root.tile-teal
             : name == "green"   ? root.tile-green
             : name == "lime"    ? root.tile-lime
             : name == "yellow"  ? root.tile-yellow
             : name == "amber"   ? root.tile-amber
             : name == "orange"  ? root.tile-orange
             : name == "red"     ? root.tile-red
             : name == "pink"    ? root.tile-pink
             : name == "magenta" ? root.tile-magenta
             : name == "violet"  ? root.tile-violet
             : name == "slate"   ? root.tile-slate
             // An app we have no colour for gets a quiet tile, not the accent: a solid accent
             // block would make every unknown app the loudest thing in the launcher.
             : Theme.bg-elevated;
    }

    /// The glyph colour that reads on a hue's tile.
    public pure function on-tile(name: string) -> color {
        return name == "lime" || name == "yellow" || name == "amber" ? root.tile-ink
             : name == "" ? Theme.text-secondary
             : #ffffff;
    }

    /// An app's glyph tone, on a surface. The call every existing site uses.
    public pure function for-app(id: string) -> color {
        return root.hue(root.hue-for-app(id));
    }

    /// The same colour as a surface behind it: the badge a glyph sits in.
    ///
    /// One derivation rather than a second hand-written list, so a colour and its tint can
    /// never disagree.
    public pure function tint-for-app(id: string) -> color {
        return root.for-app(id).transparentize(ThemeMode.dark ? 0.86 : 0.90);
    }

    /// An app's solid icon tile. For AppTile; nowhere else should be this loud.
    public pure function tile-for-app(id: string) -> color {
        return root.tile-hue(root.hue-for-app(id));
    }

    /// The glyph colour on an app's solid tile.
    public pure function on-tile-for-app(id: string) -> color {
        return root.on-tile(root.hue-for-app(id));
    }
}

/// Which app this window belongs to, and the two colours that follow from it.
///
/// Set once, by AppHeader, from the app-id every app already gives it -- so no app has to say
/// what it is twice and none of them can drift.
///
/// # Why identity and not the accent
///
/// The obvious move is to make Theme.accent itself app-coloured and let all seven hundred
/// existing call sites follow. That is wrong, and the palette above is the reason: these hues
/// were tuned as ICON TINTS on a dark surface. Theme.accent is also a FILL -- the background of
/// a primary button, with light text on it -- and #e8c44a behind white text is unreadable. One
/// token cannot be both.
///
/// So the split is the one macOS makes: colour is identity, the accent is action. Notes wears
/// yellow in its header, its sidebar selection and its empty state; the button that creates a
/// note is still the system accent, because "this is the primary action" is not a fact about
/// Notes. Anything added here has to pass the same test -- is this saying WHICH app, or is it
/// saying WHAT this control does?
export global AppIdentity {
    in-out property <string> id: "";

    out property <color> accent: root.id != "" ? AppColor.for-app(root.id) : Theme.accent;
    out property <color> tint:   root.id != "" ? AppColor.tint-for-app(root.id) : Theme.tint-accent;
}
